 ],
 "literal": "The roar of the sea having subsided, the seashore grove having become dim, / the water of the port in the dark backwater has become desolate. / Living in the beautiful common ground joining with the palm leaves of the palmyra palm, / the anril bird also cries gently. Today, will he / come, may you live, my friend, we suffering / even if we sulk, fearing separation like that, / he who has subdued the hard-to-leave passion?",
 "translation": "The roar of the sea has subsided, and the seashore grove has grown dim. The dark backwaters of the port have become desolate. Living among the palm leaves of the palmyra tree in the common ground, the anril bird cries softly. My friend, may you live long! Will he return today—he who has subdued his hard-to-leave passion, fearing that we would suffer from separation even if we were only to sulk?",
 "modern": "கடலின் ஒலி அடங்கி, கடற்கரைச் சோலை இருண்டு, துறைமுகத்தின் கரிய கழியில் உள்ள நீர் பொலிவிழந்து காணப்படுகிறது. மன்றத்தில் உள்ள பனைமரத்தின் மடல்களில் வாழும் அன்றில் பறவையும் மெதுவாகக் குரல் எழுப்புகிறது. தோழியே, நீ வாழ்க! நாம் ஊடல் கொண்டாலும் அதனால் வரும் பிரிவுக்கு அஞ்சி, நீங்குவதற்கு அரிய காமத்தை அடக்கிக் கொண்டவர் இன்று வருவாரோ?",
 "explanation": "The friend (தோழி) is speaking to the heroine (தலைவி) during the onset of evening, which is the characteristic time of longing in the Neytal (coastal) landscape. The natural elements of the coast—the quietened sea, the dimming grove, and the desolate backwaters—reflect the heroine's inner state of despair. The palmyra tree (பெண்ணை) and the crying of the anril bird (அன்றில்), known for its inseparable mate, intensify the pain of separation. The friend wonders if the hero, who has controlled his intense passion out of fear of causing them pain through separation, will return today. Key words include 'அன்றில்' (the ibis bird), 'புல்லென்றன்றே' (has become desolate), and 'தணப்பருங் காமம்' (hard-to-leave passion).",
